#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f45f69-d8e7-5e79-a1ec-38d8912cbc19" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdf77469-6c2a-5339-b216-3bc5e2316e4d" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c3e092-d9ff-5dc9-ad1e-f31aa2b226ba" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f519fa9-de89-5b00-aa02-312cf8e32cba" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd2ff75-3253-5df6-ac32-55d5e3a95cf5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6750 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/id #uuid "633202af-a5bd-58ac-a806-89458305a7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6751 :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "27f305da-f09b-53e5-a8b8-a30d29eb0f15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6821 :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"}]}
